var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Main-functions-and-types","page":"Main functions and types","text":"Most uses of the package need only what is on this page.","title":"Main functions and types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Setting-up-a-problem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Setting up a problem"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Estimating","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Estimating"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#The-result-type","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"The result type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Errors","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ODEParameterEstimation","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ODEParameterEstimation\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of an ODE model from time-series data.\n\nBuild a ParameterEstimationProblem from a ModelingToolkit system and your data, call estimate, and read the ParameterEstimationResults it returns. The manual is at https://orebas.github.io/ODEParameterEstimation.jl/dev/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ODEParameterEstimation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem-Tuple{ModelingToolkitBase.AbstractSystem, AbstractVector{<:Symbolics.Equation}}","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationProblem(system, measured_quantities; data, true_values, name, solver)\n\nSet up an estimation problem from a ModelingToolkit System and the quantities that were measured.\n\nmeasured_quantities is a vector of equations such as [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x1 + x2]. Each left-hand side names one measured series; each right-hand side says what it measures in terms of the model's states and parameters.\n\nKeywords\n\ndata: the measurements. A NamedTuple, a dictionary, or anything with named columns such as a DataFrame, with the time points under t and one vector per measured quantity under its name, for example (t = times, y1 = first_series, y2 = second_series). All series share the time points. For series measured at different times, pass an ObservationData. Leave it out to simulate data with sample_problem_data.\ntrue_values: the parameter values and initial conditions that generated the data, as pairs such as [a => 0.4, x1 => 1.0], when they are known. They are needed only to simulate data and to report errors against the truth. Numbers the system already carries, as in @parameters a = 0.4, are used for anything not listed.\nname: a label for the problem. Defaults to the system's name.\nsolver: the ODE solver used to simulate the model while estimating. Defaults to AutoVern9(Rodas5P()). sample_problem_data replaces it with its ode_solver option.\n\nExample\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named model = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(model, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = first_series, y2 = second_series))\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationProblem\n\nA model, the quantities measured on it, and the measurements.\n\nBuild one from a ModelingToolkit System with ParameterEstimationProblem(system, measured_quantities; data). The example models in ODEParameterEstimation.Examples, such as lotka_volterra(), return ready-made problems without data.\n\nFields\n\nname: a label for the problem.\nmodel: the system, as an OrderedODESystem.\nmeasured_quantities: equations such as y1 ~ x1, one per measured series.\ndata_sample: the measurements, or nothing before any are attached. Either a dictionary with the time points under \"t\" and each series under the right-hand side of its measured quantity, or an ObservationData.\nsolver: the ODE solver used to simulate the model while estimating.\np_true, ic: the true parameter values and initial conditions, NaN where they are not known.\nrecommended_time_interval, unident_count: notes carried by the built-in example models. Estimation does not read them.\n\nThe struct can also be built by passing all nine fields in the order name, model, measured_quantities, data_sample, recommended_time_interval, solver, p_true, ic, unident_count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.sample_problem_data","page":"Main functions and types","text":"sample_problem_data(problem; options...) -> ParameterEstimationProblem\nsample_problem_data(problem, options::EstimationOptions)\n\nSimulate measurements for problem from its true parameter values and initial conditions, and return a copy of the problem that carries them.\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 51, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0], noise_level = 0.01)\n\nThe options that matter here are:\n\ndatasize: the number of time points.\ntime_interval: [start, stop]; the points are evenly spaced over it. An example model from ODEParameterEstimation.Examples has an interval of its own, which the keyword form uses when you give none.\nuneven_sampling_times (experimental): if not empty, use these time points instead of time_interval. There must be datasize of them, in increasing order. The initial conditions are the values at the first of them.\nnoise_level and noise_model: add Gaussian noise. :additive uses a standard deviation of noise_level times the mean absolute value of each series; :relative scales each value by 1 + noise_level * randn(); :none adds none.\node_solver, abstol, reltol: the ODE solver and tolerances for the simulation. The returned problem keeps ode_solver as its solver.\nseed: with an integer, the noise is the same on every call and the caller's default RNG is left untouched. With nothing (the default), noise is drawn from the caller's default RNG, so Random.seed! controls it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.sample_problem_data"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationData","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ObservationData(series; initial_time = 0.0)\n\nMeasurements in which each series has its own time points. Pass one as data to ParameterEstimationProblem in place of a table.\n\nArguments\n\nseries: a vector of ObservationSeries.\ninitial_time: the time at which initial conditions are reported. It cannot be later than the first measurement.\n\nThe series have to overlap in time, because the equations are solved inside the interval that all of them cover. Gaps are not filled in and repeated measurements are not averaged. Uncertainty (compute_uncertainty) is not available for data of this kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationData"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationSeries","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ObservationSeries(name, experiment, quantity, times, values; noise_std = nothing)\n\nOne measured series with its own time points.\n\nArguments\n\nname: a label for the series.\nexperiment: a label for the experiment it belongs to.\nquantity: what was measured, as an expression in the model's states, such as x1 or x1 + x2.\ntimes, values: the measurements. They are copied and sorted by time. A time may appear more than once.\nnoise_std: the standard deviation of the noise in each value, if you know it. It is kept with the series. Estimation does not use it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationSeries"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.observation_times","page":"Main functions and types","text":"observation_times(data, quantity)\n\nThe times at which quantity was measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.observation_times"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.estimate","page":"Main functions and types","text":"estimate(problem; options...) -> Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}\nestimate(problem, options::EstimationOptions)\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of problem from its data.\n\nReturns the solutions found, best fit first. There is usually one. There are several when the data cannot tell them apart, and each then fits equally well. The vector is empty if no solution was found.\n\nKeyword arguments are the fields of EstimationOptions, for example estimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true).\n\nThese are the returned_results of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem, without the states the package adds for inputs such as sin(c * t). Call that function instead when you also want the error statistics against known true values or the uncertainty report, which is experimental.\n\nExample\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nbest = first(results)\nbest.parameters      # OrderedDict of parameter => value\nbest.states          # initial conditions\nbest[a]              # one value, by symbol or by name (best[:a])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.estimate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_parameter_estimation_problem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, options = EstimationOptions())\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of problem and return everything the run produced, as (raw_results, analysis, uq).\n\nestimate is the short form: it returns analysis.returned_results, without the states the package adds for inputs such as sin(c * t).\n\nReturns\n\nraw_results: every candidate solution the solver found, before ranking, with the identifiability findings. Mostly useful for debugging.\nanalysis: a named tuple with these fields, in this order, so it can also be unpacked by position:\nreturned_results: the solutions, best fit first, as ParameterEstimationResults.\nbesterror, best_min_error, best_mean_error, best_median_error, best_max_error: relative errors against the problem's true values, leaving out the quantities the data cannot determine, each the best over all candidates; besterror is the same as best_max_error. They are Inf when the true values are not known.\nbest_approximation_error: the fit error of the best solution.\nbest_rms_error: the root-mean-square relative error, as above.\nalgebraic_multiplicity: the number of solutions the equations admit, when it was determined.\nuq: with compute_uncertainty = true, an UncertaintyReport, or an UncertaintyUnavailable that says why there is none; otherwise nothing. Uncertainty estimation is experimental.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_parameter_estimation_problem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationOptions","page":"Main functions and types","text":"EstimationOptions(; options...)\n\nEverything that can be adjusted about an estimation. Every option has a default, and the defaults are meant to be used: estimate(problem) is the normal call.\n\nOptions are given by keyword, either directly or through this struct:\n\nestimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true)\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(seed = 1, progress = true)\nestimate(problem, options)\n\nThe options most people reach for come first. The rest adjust one stage of the method each, and are grouped by stage.\n\nOptions marked experimental are for studying the method. They may change or be removed in a minor release. The others change only in a major release.\n\nEveryday options\n\ninterpolators: the curve fits used to estimate derivatives of the data, as a vector such as [InterpolatorAAA]. Each is run and their candidate solutions are pooled. The default runs nine: Gaussian processes, rational approximations and Chebyshev series. A shorter list is faster. InterpolatorAAA, a rational approximation, suits exact or nearly exact data; InterpolatorGP, a Gaussian process, suits noisy data; InterpolatorGPUncertainty is needed for compute_uncertainty; and InterpolatorCustom uses a function of your own.\npolish_solutions (default true): refine each solution by least squares against the data. false returns the algebraic estimates as they are.\nopt_lb, opt_ub (default nothing): lower and upper bounds, as vectors with the states first and then the parameters. Refinement stays inside them. They cannot yet be used with a model that has an input such as sin(c * t).\nseed (default nothing): with an integer, repeated runs give identical results and Julia's default random number generator is left as it was. With nothing, random draws come from the default generator, so results vary a little from run to run unless you call Random.seed! first.\nprogress (default false): print one timestamped line as each stage starts and finishes.\nshooting_points (default 12): how many time points the equations are solved at. Fewer is faster.\nabstol, reltol (default 1e-14): tolerances for solving the model's ODEs, both when simulating data and during refinement and the other steps that integrate the model.\n\nSimulated data\n\nThese are read by sample_problem_data, which also uses abstol, reltol and seed.\n\ndatasize (default 21): the number of time points.\ntime_interval (default [-0.5, 0.5]): the first and last time.\nnoise_level (default 0.0): the size of the Gaussian noise added.\nnoise_model (default :additive): with :additive, the noise has standard deviation noise_level times the mean absolute value of the series. With :relative, each value is multiplied by 1 + noise_level * randn(). :none adds no noise.\nuneven_sampling_times (default empty, experimental): if not empty, sample at these times, of which there must be datasize, instead of evenly over time_interval. The initial conditions are the values at the first of them.\node_solver (default AutoVern9(Rodas5P())): the ODE solver for the simulation. The problem sample_problem_data returns keeps it as its solver for the estimation.\n\nOutput\n\nnooutput (default true): print nothing and pass only errors to the logger. false prints the stages and a summary of the results.\ndiagnostics (default false): print detailed diagnostics and write the candidate-synthesis log under artifacts/diagnostics/.\nsave_system (default false), save_filepath (default \"\"): save the polynomial systems to files, under save_filepath or else saved_systems/.\nprofile_phases (default false, experimental): print the time and memory each stage took.\nhc_show_progress (default false): show HomotopyContinuation's progress bar.\n\nEstimating derivatives\n\ncustom_interpolators (default Function[]): your own functions (times, values) -> curve, one for each InterpolatorCustom entry in interpolators, in order.\nauto_filter_interpolators (default true): leave out the rational interpolators when the data look noisy, because they amplify noise.\ns3_adapt_k (default 10.0, experimental): noise multiplier for the tolerance of the two default curve fits that pass a Gaussian process through a rational approximation. Higher keeps fewer support points.\ngp_derivative_lengthscale_factor (default 1.0, experimental): multiplies the fitted length scale of InterpolatorGPUncertainty. Values below one smooth less.\n\nBuilding and solving the equations\n\nflow (default FlowStandard): FlowStandard is the algebraic method. FlowDirectOpt fits by local optimization alone.\nsystem_solver (default SolverHC): how the polynomial systems are solved. SolverHC is homotopy continuation, which finds every solution. The others are local solvers and can miss solutions: SolverTrustRegion is a trust-region method started from several points, and SolverLevenbergMarquardt is Levenberg–Marquardt from one point.\nuse_si_template (default true, experimental): has no effect. The equations are always built with StructuralIdentifiability.jl, and false is refused.\nsi_probability (default 0.99): the probability with which the identifiability analysis is correct.\nsi_fix_strategy (default :local_basis): how values are chosen for quantities that cannot be determined: :local_basis or :identifiable_functions.\nsystem_construction_policy (default :noise_frontier, experimental): which equations are used. :noise_frontier prefers systems that need lower derivatives of the data and have fewer solutions. :legacy is the earlier choice.\nconstruction_candidate_limit (default 64), construction_beam_width (default 16), construction_compute_mixed_volume (default true), experimental: limits on the search that :noise_frontier does.\nshooting_warp (default true), shooting_warp_beta (default 3.0), experimental: place more of the shooting_points near the start of the data, and how strongly.\nuse_multipoint (default true): also solve systems that combine equations from several time points.\nmultipoint_n_points (default 2), multipoint_max_pairs (default 20), multipoint_pair_strategy (default :spread), experimental: how many time points each of those systems combines, how many combinations are tried, and how they are chosen, :spread or :boundary_order.\nauto_handle_transcendentals (default true): rewrite sin(c*t), cos(c*t) and exp(c*t) in polynomial form.\nauto_rescale (default true): rescale states, parameters and data by powers of two to bring them near one, and convert the estimates back.\nuse_parameter_homotopy (default true): solve at one time point and track the solutions to the others, which is faster than solving each afresh.\nhomotopy_tracking_mode (default :generic_start, experimental): how that tracking is done: :generic_start, :gamma_straight, :gamma_straight_fallback or :parameter.\ngamma_max_seeds (default 5), gamma_seed (default 0), experimental: the number of random restarts for the tracking, and their seed. 0 uses a fixed seed for each problem, a positive value is used as given, and a negative value draws a new one every run, which seed cannot make repeatable.\nuse_column_scaling (default true, experimental): scale the unknowns of each polynomial system by the size of the data derivatives.\nhc_real_tol (default 1e-9, experimental): how small the imaginary part of a solution must be for it to count as real.\nhc_threading (default true): let HomotopyContinuation use threads.\nhc_compile_mode (default :all, experimental): whether HomotopyContinuation compiles the systems: :all, :none or :mixed.\n\nChoosing the solutions to return\n\nalgebraic_multiplicity (default nothing): the number of solutions the equations have. It is worked out automatically unless you set it. At most this many results are returned.\ncompute_algebraic_multiplicity (default true, experimental): false skips working it out.\nbranch_top_k (default 20): the most results returned when that number is not known. 0 returns all of them.\nbranch_detection (default true, experimental): rank without using the true values. false ranks by distance to them, which is only meaningful in a benchmark.\nclustering_threshold (default 1e-5, experimental): the relative distance below which two candidates are the same solution.\nrough_cluster_eps (default 1.0), subspace_cluster_eps (default 0.05), experimental: how near-duplicate results are merged. Candidates that are far apart are separated first, then close ones within each group are merged, at these two distances.\nbranch_cluster_eps (default 0.001), branch_err_factor (default 100.0), experimental: before refinement, drop candidates whose fit error is more than branch_err_factor times the best, and merge the rest at this distance.\nbranch_diversity_selection (default true), branch_diversity_eps (default 0.01), experimental: when several results are returned, prefer ones that differ by at least this relative distance.\nbranch_completion (default true): when the equations have several solutions, take the best one found and solve for the others from it.\nbranch_completion_max_anchors (default 1), branch_completion_residual_tol (default 1e-6), experimental: how many of the best solutions to start from, and the residual a new solution must reach.\nsynthesize_aggregate_candidates (default true): add candidates made by combining the others: the median, mean and trimmed mean of each value.\n\nRefining solutions\n\npolish_solver_solutions (default true): refine each solution of the polynomial equations against those equations.\npolish_solver_jacobian (default :forwarddiff), polish_solver_chunk_size (default 1), experimental: how the Jacobian for that is computed, :forwarddiff, :symbolic or :finitediff, and ForwardDiff's chunk size, where 0 chooses automatically.\npolish_method (default PolishLeastSquares): the optimizer for polish_solutions. PolishLeastSquares is Levenberg–Marquardt. PolishBFGS and PolishLBFGS minimize the sum of squares directly.\npolish_maxiters (default 100), polish_maxtime (default 3600.0 seconds), polish_max_concurrency (default Threads.nthreads()): limits on the iterations and time spent on each solution, and on how many are refined at once.\npolish_divergence_factor (default 10.0), polish_stagnation_window (default 50), experimental: stop when the loss has grown by this factor, or has not improved for this many iterations.\npolish_ode_maxiters (default 5000, experimental): the step limit for the ODE solver during refinement.\npolish_coordinate_policy (default :auto, experimental): the coordinates refinement works in. :auto takes logarithms of quantities whose bounds are positive. The others are :linear, :log_only and :shifted_log_only.\npolish_regularization_lambda (default 0.0, experimental): the strength of an L2 penalty in those coordinates.\npolish_softwall_lambda (default 1e-2), polish_softwall_epsilon (default 0.10), experimental: a penalty that keeps values off their bounds: its strength, and the width of the band beside each bound where it acts, as a fraction of the range.\npolish_lso_delta (default 10.0), polish_lso_x_tol, polish_lso_f_tol, polish_lso_g_tol (default -1.0), experimental: the trust-region radius and tolerances of PolishLeastSquares. A negative tolerance means abstol or reltol.\nopt_maxiters (default 10000): the iteration limit for the local optimization of FlowDirectOpt.\nopt_ad_backend (default :forward, experimental): how the optimizers get derivatives: :forward for ForwardDiff or :finite for finite differences.\nterminal_fallback (default :direct_opt): what to do when the algebraic method finds nothing. :direct_opt fits by local optimization, within polish_maxiters and polish_maxtime. :none returns no results.\nbacksolve_recovery (default :algebraic_resolve): what to do when a solution blows up as it is integrated back to the first time point. :algebraic_resolve keeps its parameters and solves for the states at that time again. :none does nothing.\nuse_sensitivity_seeds (default false), sensitivity_seed_probe_scale (default 1.0), sensitivity_seed_eigenvalue_threshold (default 0.01), sensitivity_seed_mahalanobis_threshold (default -1.0), sensitivity_sigma_d_kappa (default 2.0), experimental: add starting points for refinement along the directions in which the data constrain a solution least.\n\nUncertainty\n\nAll of these are experimental.\n\ncompute_uncertainty (default false): also estimate standard errors for the best solution. Needs InterpolatorGPUncertainty in interpolators.\nuq_failure_policy (default :return_failed): when standard errors cannot be computed, :return_failed returns an UncertaintyUnavailable that says why, and :throw throws an ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyError.\nuq_noise_source (default :learned_gp_homoscedastic): where the noise level comes from: the Gaussian process fit, or with :smoother_residual_edf the residuals of that fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationResult","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationResult\n\nOne solution: parameter values and initial conditions with which the model reproduces the data.\n\nPrinting a result shows its values. result[a], result[:a] and result[\"a\"] look one up.\n\nFields\n\nparameters: the estimated parameter values, keyed by symbol.\nstates: the estimated states at report_time, keyed by symbol.\nreport_time: the time those states belong to: the first time point of the data, or the initial_time of an ObservationData.\nerr: the fit error, the sum of squared differences between the data and the simulated model over all series and time points, after each series is scaled by a power of two to be of order one (unless auto_rescale = false). Results are ranked by it.\nall_unidentifiable: the parameters and initial conditions that the measured quantities cannot determine. Their reported values are arbitrary.\nunident_dict: the values those were fixed at while solving.\nsolution: the simulated trajectory.\ndatasize: the number of time points.\nat_time: the time point at which the equations were solved.\ninterpolator_source: the interpolator whose derivative estimates led here.\nprovenance: a ResultProvenance record of how the solution was produced.\nreturn_code: an older summary of the same: :algebraic, :direct_opt, or the name of a recovery path.\nbranch_size: how many candidate solutions were merged into this one.\n\nWhen the package rescales a badly scaled problem internally (auto_rescale), err, solution and unident_dict stay in the rescaled units. parameters and states are always in yours.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationResult"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{ParameterEstimationResult, Any}","page":"Main functions and types","text":"result[variable]\n\nThe estimated value of a parameter, or of a state's initial condition. variable is the symbol, or its name: result[a], result[:a] and result[\"a\"] are the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedModelClassError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"UnsupportedModelClassError\n\nThrown when a model is not rational: it applies a function such as sin, exp or sqrt to a state or to an unknown parameter. expressions lists the terms found, and category says which kind they are.\n\nsin(c * t), cos(c * t) and exp(c * t) with a number c depend on time alone and are accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedModelClassError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError\n\nThrown when a model needs a higher derivative of the measured data than the package computes (order 20). This happens when a few measured quantities have to account for many states and parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.SamplingFailureError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"SamplingFailureError\n\nThrown by sample_problem_data when the model cannot be simulated over the whole time interval, for example because the solution blows up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.SamplingFailureError"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#How-it-works","page":"How it works","text":"Most parameter-estimation tools search. They start from a guess, simulate the model, and adjust the guess to shrink the mismatch with the data. That works when the guess is good. When it is not, the search can settle on a wrong answer, and nothing tells you whether a different answer would fit just as well.\n\nThis package solves equations instead.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#The-idea","page":"How it works","text":"Take a model with one parameter and one measured quantity:\n\nx(t) = -ax(t) qquad y(t) = x(t)\n\nDifferentiating the measurement gives y = x = -ay. So if the data tell us y and y at some time, then a = -yy. That is an equation for a, and solving it takes no search.\n\nThe same thing works in general. Differentiate the measured quantities a few times, use the model to rewrite each derivative of a state, and you get polynomial equations that link the unknown parameters and states to the values of y y y dots at a chosen time. Take enough derivatives and there are as many equations as unknowns.","title":"The idea"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#The-steps","page":"How it works","text":"Find out what can be determined. StructuralIdentifiability.jl works out, from the model alone, which parameters and initial conditions the measured quantities determine. Anything they do not determine is given a fixed value and flagged in the result.\nEstimate derivatives from the data. Each measured series is fitted with a smooth curve, and the curve is differentiated. Several kinds of curve are used: Gaussian processes, which cope with noise, and rational and Chebyshev approximations, which are very accurate on clean data.\nBuild the polynomial equations and solve them. The derivative values go into the equations, at several time points and for each fitted curve. HomotopyContinuation.jl finds all the solutions of each system.\nTest every candidate against the data. Each real solution gives parameter values and initial conditions. The model is simulated with them and compared with the measurements.\nRefine. The candidates that come close are then fitted to the data by least squares, each starting from its own values. On exact data this changes nothing. On noisy data it is what makes the answer accurate.\nReport the distinct answers. Candidates that agree are merged, and the rest are ranked by how well they fit. The package works out how many solutions the equations have and returns at most that many.","title":"The steps"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#What-follows-from-this","page":"How it works","text":"Every answer, not one. If two parameter sets explain the data equally well, you get both. See More than one answer.\nRational models only. The equations must be polynomial, so the model has to be built from polynomials and their ratios. See Which models work.\nDerivatives are the hard part. Noise grows with every derivative taken, so on noisy data the solutions of the equations are rough, and the refinement step does the rest. See Noisy data.","title":"What follows from this"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#Papers","page":"How it works","text":"The method is described in\n\nO. Bassik, Y. Berman, S. Go, H. Hong, I. Ilmer, A. Ovchinnikov, C. Rackauckas, P. Soto, C. Yap. Robust parameter estimation for rational ordinary differential equations. Applied Mathematics and Computation 509 (2026). doi:10.1016/j.amc.2025.129638, arXiv:2303.02159.\n\nIts extension to noisy data, which estimates the derivatives with Gaussian processes, is described in\n\nO. Bassik, A. Demin, A. Ovchinnikov. Practical algebraic parameter estimation for noisy data via Gaussian process regression. arXiv:2609.30451 (2026).","title":"Papers"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This page fits a model to data from start to finish. Most of the time it takes is Julia installing and compiling packages.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installing","page":"Getting started","text":"You need Julia 1.12 or later. Add the package along with ModelingToolkit, which models are written in:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"ODEParameterEstimation\")\nPkg.add(\"ModelingToolkit\")","title":"Installing"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#The-whole-thing","page":"Getting started","text":"using ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n# A predator-prey model with four unknown rates.\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\n# Both populations were counted. Here the counts are simulated from known values.\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    true_values = [α => 1.1, β => 0.4, γ => 0.4, δ => 0.1, prey => 1.0, predators => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    α = 1.1\n    β = 0.4\n    γ = 0.4\n    δ = 0.1\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    prey(t) = 1\n    predators(t) = 0.5\n  Fit error: 5.86e-26\n\nThe estimates are the values the data were simulated from.","title":"The whole thing"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#What-each-part-does","page":"Getting started","text":"The model is an ordinary ModelingToolkit System. t is time and D is the derivative with respect to it. If you have a model written for simulation with ModelingToolkit, you can pass that same system.\n\nThe measured quantities, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators], say what was observed. Each left-hand side is a name you choose for one series of measurements. Each right-hand side says what that series measures. It does not have to be a single state: if only the total number of animals had been counted, you would write [y ~ prey + predators].\n\nThe data here come from sample_problem_data, which simulates the model with the true_values and records the measured quantities at 101 times. With real measurements you skip both and pass the numbers themselves:\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = prey_counts, y2 = predator_counts))\n\nestimate returns a vector of results, best fit first. There is usually one. More than one answer explains when there are several.\n\nThe first time a model is estimated in a Julia session, Julia compiles code for that model. For this one that takes under a minute. Calling it again takes seconds, with the same data or with new data.","title":"What each part does"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Reading-the-result","page":"Getting started","text":"A result prints its values, and you can take them out one at a time or all together:\n\nbest = results[1]\nbest[α]\n1.1000000000000307\n\nbest.parameters\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 4 entries:\n  α => 1.1\n  β => 0.4\n  γ => 0.4\n  δ => 0.1\n\nbest.states holds the initial conditions in the same form. Both are dictionaries keyed by the symbols of your model.","title":"Reading the result"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Seeing-the-fit","page":"Getting started","text":"To see how well an estimate explains the data, simulate the model with it and draw both. This is plain ModelingToolkit, OrdinaryDiffEq and Plots, which you may need to add first:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\n\nfitted = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(lotka_volterra), merge(best.states, best.parameters), (0.0, 10.0)), Tsit5())\n\ndata = problem.data_sample   # the simulated measurements\nscatter(data[\"t\"], [data[prey] data[predators]]; label = [\"prey, measured\" \"predators, measured\"], markersize = 2)\nplot!(fitted; idxs = [prey, predators], label = [\"prey, fitted\" \"predators, fitted\"], xlabel = \"time\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}","title":"Seeing the fit"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Where-next","page":"Getting started","text":"Your own data: vectors, tables, and series measured at different times.\nNoisy data: what to expect when the measurements are not exact, and what helps.\nMore than one answer: models where the data allow several parameter sets, or leave some parameters open.\nWhich models work: the kinds of equations the method can handle.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Results","page":"Results","text":"estimate returns a vector of ParameterEstimationResults, best fit first.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.4, b => 0.9, x1 => 1.0, x2 => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n1-element Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}:\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 0.4, b = 0.9; x1(t) = 1, x2(t) = 0.5)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#One-result","page":"Results","text":"best = results[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 1\n    x2(t) = 0.5\n  Fit error: 6.47e-26\n\nThe printout has the parameters, the initial conditions with the time they belong to, and the fit error. The values can be taken out by symbol or by name:\n\nbest[a], best[:b], best[\"x1\"]\n(0.39999999999999925, 0.8999999999999745, 0.9999999999999978)\n\nor all at once, as dictionaries keyed by symbol:\n\nbest.parameters\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 2 entries:\n  a => 0.4\n  b => 0.9\n\nbest.states\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 2 entries:\n  x1(t) => 1.0\n  x2(t) => 0.5\n\nbest.err is the fit error: the sum of squared differences between the data and the model simulated with these values, after each series is scaled by a power of two to be of order one (unless auto_rescale = false). It is what results are ranked by. Use it to compare the results of one run with each other.","title":"One result"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Using-the-estimates","page":"Results","text":"best.states and best.parameters are in the form ModelingToolkit takes, so simulating the fitted model is one line:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nfitted = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(oscillator), merge(best.states, best.parameters), (0.0, 5.0)), Tsit5())\n\nSeeing the fit plots such a simulation against the data.","title":"Using the estimates"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Several-results","page":"Results","text":"When the vector holds more than one result, each is a different set of values that fits the data. When a result marks some values not identifiable, the data do not determine them. More than one answer covers both.\n\nAn empty vector means no solution was found. See Troubleshooting.","title":"Several results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#When-the-true-values-are-known","page":"Results","text":"For testing a model or the method, analyze_parameter_estimation_problem returns the results together with a comparison against the problem's true values:\n\nraw, analysis, uq = analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, EstimationOptions())\nanalysis.best_max_error\n6.750155989720952e-14\n\nanalysis.returned_results is what estimate returns, except that estimate leaves out the states the package adds for inputs such as sin(c * t). best_max_error is the largest relative error over the parameters and initial conditions the data can determine, for the candidate solution that came closest to the truth. That candidate is usually the first result, but it does not have to be, so to score the first result itself, compare its values with the truth directly.","title":"When the true values are known"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Where-a-result-came-from","page":"Results","text":"Each result records how it was produced. best.interpolator_source names the curve fit whose derivative estimates led to it, by a short internal label such as :agp_robust for InterpolatorGP, and best.provenance holds the rest: which time point it was solved at and whether it was refined afterwards.","title":"Where a result came from"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Example-models","page":"Example models","text":"The package comes with ready-made problems to try things on. They live in a submodule, ODEParameterEstimation.Examples. Each is a function that returns a ParameterEstimationProblem with true values and no data, so the usual pattern is:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(lotka_volterra(); datasize = 101)\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nEvery model carries a time interval that suits it, and sample_problem_data uses it unless you pass time_interval yourself.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nfunction describe(group)\n    println(rpad(\"model\", 32), \"states  parameters  measured  interval\")\n    for name in example_models(group)\n        problem = example_problem(name)\n        println(rpad(name, 32), rpad(length(problem.ic), 8), rpad(length(problem.p_true), 12),\n            rpad(length(problem.measured_quantities), 10), problem.recommended_time_interval)\n    end\nend","title":"Example models"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Looking-inside-one","page":"Example models","text":"A problem carries its ModelingToolkit system, its measured quantities and its true values:\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\n\nproblem = lotka_volterra()\nequations(problem.model.system)\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Equation}:\n Differential(t, 1)(r(t)) ~ k1*r(t) - k2*r(t)*w(t)\n Differential(t, 1)(w(t)) ~ -k3*w(t) + k2*r(t)*w(t)\n\nproblem.measured_quantities\n1-element Vector{Symbolics.Equation}:\n y1(t) ~ r(t)\n\nproblem.p_true\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 3 entries:\n  k1 => 1.0\n  k2 => 0.5\n  k3 => 0.3","title":"Looking inside one"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#First-steps","page":"Example models","text":"Small models that are solved quickly and accurately.\n\ndescribe(:first_steps)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nharmonic_oscillator             2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\noscillator_mixed_outputs        2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nexponential_decay               1       1           1         [0.0, 10.0]\ndecay_cubed_output              1       1           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nforced_decay                    1       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nlotka_volterra                  2       3           1         [0.0, 20.0]\nvanderpol                       2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nbrusselator                     2       2           2         [0.0, 20.0]\ndaisy_mamil3                    3       5           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nrepressilator                   6       3           3         [0.0, 24.0]","title":"First steps"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Something-cannot-be-determined","page":"Example models","text":"In each of these the measured quantities leave at least one parameter or initial condition open. See More than one answer.\n\ndescribe(:cannot_be_determined)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nsum_of_rates                    1       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\npartly_observed_chain           3       4           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nhidden_scale                    3       3           1         [0.0, 10.0]\ntreatment                       4       5           2         [0.0, 40.0]\ntwo_compartment_pk              2       5           1         [0.0, 48.0]\nmass_spring_damper              2       4           1         [0.0, 10.0]","title":"Something cannot be determined"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Several-answers","page":"Example models","text":"Two models in which several sets of values fit the data equally well, each beside a version that is measured fully enough to have one answer.\n\ndescribe(:several_answers)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nhidden_subpopulations           5       6           3         [0.0, 12.0]\nhidden_subpopulations_measured  5       6           5         [0.0, 12.0]\nreceptor_subtypes               3       6           2         [0.0, 8.0]\nreceptor_subtypes_measured      3       6           3         [0.0, 8.0]","title":"Several answers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Larger-models","page":"Example models","text":"Most of these are benchmarks from the identifiability literature. They take longer, some of them minutes, and two are beyond what the package solves in reasonable time at present. Their descriptions say which.\n\ndescribe(:larger_models)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ncrauste                         5       13          4         [0.0, 25.0]\ndaisy_ex3                       4       5           2         [0.0, 10.0]\ndaisy_mamil4                    4       7           3         [0.0, 10.0]\nfitzhugh_nagumo                 2       3           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nhiv                             5       10          4         [0.0, 25.0]\nbiohydrogenation                4       6           2         [0.0, 36.0]\nseir                            4       3           2         [0.0, 60.0]\nsirsforced                      5       6           2         [0.0, 30.0]\nslowfast                        6       2           4         [0.0, 10.0]\nallee_competition               2       8           2         [0.0, 10.0]","title":"Larger models"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Control-systems","page":"Example models","text":"Systems driven by a known sinusoidal input. See Inputs that depend on time.\n\ndescribe(:control_systems)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ndc_motor                        2       2           1         [0.0, 5.0]\nquadrotor_altitude              2       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nforced_lotka_volterra           2       4           2         [0.0, 5.0]\nmagnetic_levitation             3       3           1         [0.0, 5.0]\naircraft_pitch                  3       4           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nbicycle_model                   2       4           2         [0.0, 20.0]\nboost_converter                 2       3           2         [0.0, 0.5]\nbilinear_system                 2       8           2         [0.0, 15.0]","title":"Control systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#A-model-that-has-to-be-rewritten","page":"Example models","text":"One tank, written as it comes and written so that the package accepts it. See Rewriting a model so that it is accepted.\n\ndescribe(:rewriting)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ntank_level                      1       2           1         [0.0, 30.0]\ntank_level_rewritten            1       2           1         [0.0, 30.0]","title":"A model that has to be rewritten"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Listing-them","page":"Example models","text":"","title":"Listing them"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.brusselator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"brusselator()\n\nThe Brusselator, a two-species chemical oscillator with a cubic term, with both concentrations measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.brusselator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil3-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_mamil3()\n\nA linear three-compartment model with five exchange rates, a standard identifiability benchmark. Two of the three compartments are measured.\n\nAll five rates and the unmeasured initial amount are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil3"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.decay_cubed_output-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"decay_cubed_output()\n\nThe decay of exponential_decay, measured through its cube: the measured quantity is x1^3. A measured quantity may be any rational function of the states and parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.decay_cubed_output"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.exponential_decay-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"exponential_decay()\n\nOne state that decays, x1' = -a x1, measured directly. The simplest model there is: one parameter and one initial condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.exponential_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_decay-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"forced_decay()\n\nA decaying state pushed by a known sinusoid, x' = -a x + b sin(0.5 t), measured directly. The smallest model with an input that depends on time. See Inputs that depend on time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.harmonic_oscillator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"harmonic_oscillator()\n\nTwo states that drive each other, x1' = -a x2 and x2' = b x1, both measured. The smallest model with two parameters.\n\nBoth parameters and both initial conditions are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.harmonic_oscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.lotka_volterra-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"lotka_volterra()\n\nThe Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with three rates. Only the prey r is measured.\n\nThe three rates and the unmeasured initial number of predators are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.lotka_volterra"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.oscillator_mixed_outputs-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"oscillator_mixed_outputs()\n\nThe equations of harmonic_oscillator, with neither state measured on its own: the two measured quantities are 2 x1 + 0.5 x2 and 3 x1 - 0.25 x2.\n\nEverything is still recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.oscillator_mixed_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.repressilator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"repressilator()\n\nA ring of three genes, each repressing the next, with saturating repression terms of the form beta / (1 + n p). The three proteins are measured and the three mRNA levels are not.\n\nA model with parameters in denominators. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.repressilator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.vanderpol-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"vanderpol()\n\nThe van der Pol oscillator, x1' = a x2 and x2' = -x1 - b (x1^2 - 1) x2, with both states measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.vanderpol"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_scale-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_scale()\n\nTwo unmeasured states feed a third, x3' = c (x1 + x2), and only x3 is measured. Scaling x1 and x2 up and c down by the same factor changes nothing that is measured.\n\nThe rates a and b and x3(0) are determined. c, x1(0) and x2(0) are reported as undetermined. Two results are returned, one for each way of assigning the two rates to the two hidden states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.mass_spring_damper-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"mass_spring_damper()\n\nA mass on a spring with a damper, pushed by a constant unknown force: m x'' + c x' + k x = F. The position is measured.\n\nDividing the equation by m shows that only the ratios c/m, k/m and F/m matter. All four parameters are reported as undetermined, and the initial position and velocity are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.mass_spring_damper"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.partly_observed_chain-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"partly_observed_chain()\n\nThree states, two of them measured. x2 grows at the rate b + c, so only that sum is determined, and x3 is neither measured nor felt by anything that is, so d and its initial value are open.\n\na and the two measured initial conditions are recovered. b, c, d and x3(0) are reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.partly_observed_chain"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sum_of_rates-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"sum_of_rates()\n\nGrowth at the rate a + b: x1' = (a + b) x1, with x1 measured. Only the sum can be determined, so a and b are both reported as undetermined. The smallest example of that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sum_of_rates"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.treatment-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"treatment()\n\nAn epidemic model with treatment: susceptible S, infected In, treated Tr and the constant population N. Only the treated and the population size are measured.\n\nMost of the model is left open by those two measurements: a, b, d, g, In(0) and S(0) are reported as undetermined, and nu is recovered. It takes a long time with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.treatment"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.two_compartment_pk-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"two_compartment_pk()\n\nA drug that moves between a central and a peripheral compartment and is eliminated from the central one. Only the central concentration is measured.\n\nOne concentration curve cannot separate the five parameters: all of them and the peripheral initial concentration are reported as undetermined. Only the central initial concentration is recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.two_compartment_pk"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_subpopulations()\n\nAn epidemic in which the infected fall into three groups, each with its own infection rate b and recovery rate a. The susceptible, the recovered and the total number infected are measured, so the three groups are never seen apart.\n\nNothing in the data says which group is which. There are six answers, one for each ordering of the three groups, and all six fit equally well. Compare hidden_subpopulations_measured. It takes a long time with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations_measured-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_subpopulations_measured()\n\nThe model of hidden_subpopulations with each of the three infected groups measured on its own. There is one answer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations_measured"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"receptor_subtypes()\n\nA ligand L that binds two receptor subtypes, each with its own amount and its own binding and release rates. The free ligand and the total amount bound are measured.\n\nThe two subtypes can be exchanged, so there are two answers. Compare receptor_subtypes_measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes_measured-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"receptor_subtypes_measured()\n\nThe model of receptor_subtypes with the amount bound to each subtype measured on its own. There is one answer, and it is found quickly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes_measured"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.allee_competition-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"allee_competition()\n\nTwo competing species, each with logistic growth and an Allee threshold below which it declines. Both populations are measured. Eight parameters, with the carrying capacities in denominators.\n\nThere are four answers that fit the data equally well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.allee_competition"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.biohydrogenation-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"biohydrogenation()\n\nA chain of three reactions with saturating rates of the form k x / (K + x). The first two of the four concentrations are measured.\n\nThe final product x7 affects nothing that is measured, so x7(0) is reported as undetermined. There are two answers for the rest. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.biohydrogenation"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.crauste-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"crauste()\n\nThe Crauste model of the CD8 T-cell response to infection: naive cells N, early and late effector cells E and L, memory cells M and the pathogen P, with thirteen rates. N, E, P and the sum L + M are measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.crauste"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_ex3-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_ex3()\n\nA linear three-state model driven by a ramp input u0, from the examples of the DAISY identifiability software. x1 and the input are measured.\n\nThere are two answers. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_ex3"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil4-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_mamil4()\n\nA linear four-compartment model with seven exchange rates. x1, x2 and the sum x3 + x4 are measured.\n\nCompartments 3 and 4 are seen only together and can be exchanged, so there are two answers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil4"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.fitzhugh_nagumo-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"fitzhugh_nagumo()\n\nThe FitzHugh–Nagumo model of a nerve cell: a fast voltage V with a cubic term and a slow recovery variable R. Only the voltage is measured.\n\nThe three parameters and the unmeasured R(0) are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.fitzhugh_nagumo"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hiv-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hiv()\n\nA model of HIV infection and the immune response to it: uninfected cells x, infected cells y, virus v, and immune cells w and z, with ten rates. Four quantities are measured, one of them the sum y + v.\n\nThe values span eight orders of magnitude, from 2e-5 to 1000. The package rescales the problem internally (auto_rescale). It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hiv"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.seir-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"seir()\n\nThe SEIR epidemic model: susceptible S, exposed E, infected In and the constant population N. The infected and the population size are measured.\n\nThere are two answers: the data cannot tell the recovery rate a from the rate nu at which the exposed become infected. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.seir"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sirsforced-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"sirsforced()\n\nAn SIRS epidemic model whose infection rate varies with the seasons. The seasonal factor is produced by two extra states, x1 and x2, that form an oscillator of unknown frequency M. The infected and the recovered are measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sirsforced"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.slowfast-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"slowfast()\n\nA chain of two reactions, A to B to C, watched through an absorbance signal eA xA + eB xB + eC xC. The concentration of C, the signal and two of the three coefficients are measured. The coefficients are constants, written as states that do not change.\n\nThere are two answers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.slowfast"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.aircraft_pitch-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"aircraft_pitch()\n\nThe short-period pitch dynamics of an aircraft: pitch angle theta, pitch rate q and angle of attack alpha. The elevator moves as 0.05 sin(2 t) and the pitch rate is measured.\n\nThe four aerodynamic coefficients are recovered. The pitch angle itself affects nothing that is measured, so theta(0) is reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.aircraft_pitch"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bicycle_model-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"bicycle_model()\n\nThe lateral dynamics of a car as a single-track (\"bicycle\") model: lateral velocity vy and yaw rate r, with the steering angle 0.05 sin(0.5 t). Both states are measured. The geometry and the speed are taken as known.\n\nThe tyre stiffnesses Cf and Cr enter only divided by the mass or by the yaw inertia, so only those ratios are determined. All four parameters are reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bicycle_model"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bilinear_system-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"bilinear_system()\n\nA two-state system in which the input u = 1 + 0.5 sin(2 t) both adds to the states and multiplies them. Both states are measured.\n\nAll eight coefficients are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bilinear_system"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.boost_converter-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"boost_converter()\n\nThe averaged model of a boost converter: inductor current iL and capacitor voltage vC, with the duty cycle varying as 0.5 + 0.1 sin(100 t). Both states are measured and the input voltage is taken as known.\n\nThe inductance, capacitance and load resistance are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.boost_converter"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.dc_motor-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"dc_motor()\n\nA DC motor: the armature current i produces a torque that turns the rotor at speed omega_m. The supply voltage is 12 + 2 sin(5 t) and the speed is measured. The electrical constants and the torque constant are taken as known.\n\nThe rotor inertia J and the friction coefficient b are recovered. With the torque constant unknown as well, only the ratios Kt/J and b/J could be determined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.dc_motor"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_lotka_volterra-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"forced_lotka_volterra()\n\nA predator-prey model whose prey is harvested and restocked through the seasons, at the rate 0.3 sin(2 t). Both populations are measured.\n\nAll four rates are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_lotka_volterra"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.magnetic_levitation-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"magnetic_levitation()\n\nA ball held by an electromagnet, linearised about its resting point: position x, velocity v and coil current i. The coil voltage is 5 + sin(5 t) and only the position is measured. The electrical constants are taken as known.\n\nThe effective mass, stiffness and damping are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.magnetic_levitation"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.quadrotor_altitude-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"quadrotor_altitude()\n\nThe altitude z of a quadrotor with linear drag, driven by a net vertical force 2 sin(t). The altitude is measured.\n\nThe mass m and the drag coefficient d are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.quadrotor_altitude"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"tank_level()\n\nA tank that drains through a hole in its floor. The level h falls at a rate proportional to sqrt(h), and the inflow 0.4 + 0.15 sin(0.5 t) is known. The level is measured.\n\nsqrt(h) is not a rational function of the state, so estimate refuses this model with an UnsupportedModelClassError. tank_level_rewritten is the same tank in a form that is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level_rewritten-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"tank_level_rewritten()\n\nThe tank of tank_level with the state z = sqrt(h) in place of the level. Then z' = (Qin - k z) / (2 A z), which is rational, and the measured level is z^2. The data are the same.\n\nThe area A and the outflow coefficient k are recovered. A second result has z and k both negated: it gives the same level, and is the price of measuring z only through its square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level_rewritten"},{"category":"module","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples","page":"Example models","text":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nReady-made problems to try the package on. Each model is a function that returns a ParameterEstimationProblem with true values and no data:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(lotka_volterra(); datasize = 101)\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nexample_models lists them and example_groups lists the groups they are sorted into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_models","page":"Example models","text":"example_models() -> Vector{Symbol}\nexample_models(group::Symbol) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe names of the example models, all of them or those of one group.\n\nExample\n\nexample_models(:first_steps)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_models"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_groups","page":"Example models","text":"example_groups() -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe groups the example models are sorted into: :first_steps, :cannot_be_determined, :several_answers, :larger_models, :control_systems and :rewriting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_groups"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_problem","page":"Example models","text":"example_problem(name::Symbol) -> ParameterEstimationProblem\n\nThe example model called name: example_problem(:lotka_volterra) is lotka_volterra().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#Noisy-data","page":"Noisy data","text":"Measured data are noisy, and noise is hard on the equation-solving part of this method, because it works from derivatives of the data and differentiating amplifies noise. So estimate does not stop there. It takes each solution of the equations and fits it to the data by least squares. This page shows what each step contributes.","title":"Noisy data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#An-example","page":"Noisy data","text":"The predator-prey model again, with 101 measurements of each population. Noise has been added to them, with a standard deviation of 2% of each population's average size:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\ntruth = [α => 1.1, β => 0.4, γ => 0.4, δ => 0.1, prey => 1.0, predators => 0.5]\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators]; true_values = truth)\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem;\n    datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0], noise_level = 0.02, seed = 1)\n\nHere is the estimate, and beside it what the equations gave before the fit, which polish_solutions = false returns:\n\nrefined = estimate(problem; seed = 1)[1]\nrough = estimate(problem; seed = 1, polish_solutions = false)[1]\n\nprintln(\"              true     rough   refined\")\nfor (quantity, value) in truth\n    println(rpad(quantity, 13), rpad(value, 9), rpad(round(rough[quantity]; sigdigits = 3), 8), round(refined[quantity]; sigdigits = 3))\nend\n              true     rough   refined\nα            1.1      0.983   1.1\nβ            0.4      0.388   0.401\nγ            0.4      0.471   0.401\nδ            0.1      0.121   0.101\nprey(t)      1.0      1.07    0.997\npredators(t) 0.5      0.351   0.491\n\nThe rough values come from derivatives of noisy data. They are in the right region, and some are well off. The refined ones started there and were then fitted to the data, and they are within a few percent of the truth.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\n\nfit = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(lotka_volterra), merge(refined.states, refined.parameters), (0.0, 10.0)), Tsit5())\n\ndata = problem.data_sample\nscatter(data[\"t\"], [data[prey] data[predators]]; label = [\"prey, measured\" \"predators, measured\"], markersize = 2)\nplot!(fit; idxs = [prey, predators], label = [\"prey, fitted\" \"predators, fitted\"], xlabel = \"time\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}","title":"An example"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#Why-two-steps","page":"Noisy data","text":"Fitting by least squares is the standard way to estimate parameters, and its weakness is that it has to start near the answer. Solving the equations supplies that start, and because it finds every solution, the fit begins in the right place each time.\n\nOn exact data the solutions of the equations are already exact, and the fit leaves them as they are.","title":"Why two steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#What-else-helps","page":"Noisy data","text":"More points. The fit averages the noise over all of them, so more points mean a better answer.\nBounds. If you know the values are positive, or lie in some range, pass opt_lb and opt_ub. See Options.\nMeasuring more. A model whose unknowns all show up in the first or second derivative of the data gives a far better start than one that needs the fifth. Measuring one more state can make that difference.\n\nA run on noisy data can be made faster by using a single Gaussian-process interpolator, which here gives the same answer:\n\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorGP])","title":"What else helps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#How-good-is-the-answer","page":"Noisy data","text":"Plot it, as above. For a number, there is an experimental way to estimate a standard error for each parameter. See Uncertainty.","title":"How good is the answer"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Options","page":"Options","text":"estimate(problem) uses defaults that are meant to work as they are. This page covers the few options worth knowing about. EstimationOptions lists them all, and marks the experimental ones, which may change in a minor release.\n\nOptions are keywords of estimate:\n\nresults = estimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true)\n\nTo use the same options more than once, build them first:\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(seed = 1, progress = true)\nresults = estimate(problem, options)","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Watching-a-long-run","page":"Options","text":"A run prints nothing. With progress = true it prints a line as each stage starts and finishes:\n\n[14:02:11] ▶ Setup (identifiability)\n[14:02:11] ✓ Setup (identifiability) (0.5s)\n[14:02:11] ▶ SI Template (SIAN analysis)\n[14:02:17] ✓ SI Template (SIAN analysis) (5.9s)\n[14:02:17] ▶ Equation construction + Solving\n[14:02:17] ▶ interpolator 1/9 agp_robust","title":"Watching a long run"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Going-faster","page":"Options","text":"The first time a model is estimated in a Julia session, Julia compiles code for it. Nothing here changes that, and the second call shows the real speed.\n\nAfter that, most of the time goes into solving the polynomial equations once for every curve fit and every time point. Two options reduce the count.\n\ninterpolators chooses the curve fits. The default uses nine, and one is often enough:\n\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorAAA])   # exact or nearly exact data\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorGP])    # noisy data\n\nshooting_points is the number of time points the equations are solved at. The default is 12.\n\nestimate(problem; shooting_points = 4)\n\nBoth trade some reliability for speed. The best answer is chosen from all the attempts, and with fewer attempts there is less to choose from.","title":"Going faster"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Refining-estimates-from-noisy-data","page":"Options","text":"Each answer is refined by least squares against the data, starting from the algebraic estimate. Noisy data shows what that buys. polish_solutions = false turns it off and returns the algebraic estimates as they are.\n\nIf you know the range the values must lie in, opt_lb and opt_ub keep the refinement inside it. Each is a vector with the states first and the parameters after them, in the order ModelingToolkit lists them: unknowns(problem.model.system), then parameters(problem.model.system). Bounds cannot yet be used with a model that has an input such as sin(c * t): the package adds states to such a model, and the vectors no longer line up.","title":"Refining estimates from noisy data"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Repeatable-results","page":"Options","text":"Several steps draw random numbers, so two runs on the same data can differ in the last digits, and now and then in which answer comes first. A seed makes runs repeat exactly:\n\nestimate(problem; seed = 1)\n\nsample_problem_data takes the same option, to fix the noise it adds. The seed does not touch Julia's default random number generator, so your own rand calls are unaffected.","title":"Repeatable results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Standard-errors","page":"Options","text":"compute_uncertainty = true adds standard errors for the best answer. This part of the package is experimental. See Uncertainty.","title":"Standard errors"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#The-ODE-solver","page":"Options","text":"Candidate answers are tested by simulating the model. The solver for that belongs to the problem:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(model, measured; data, solver = Rodas5P())\n\nThe default, AutoVern9(Rodas5P()), switches to a stiff method when it needs to. abstol and reltol, both 1e-14 by default, are its tolerances.\n\nWhen the data come from sample_problem_data, its ode_solver option (same default) simulates them and becomes the problem's solver, replacing any given to the constructor.","title":"The ODE solver"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Seeing-what-the-package-is-doing","page":"Options","text":"nooutput = false prints the package's own account of a run: the stages, the candidates and a summary table. diagnostics = true adds much more and writes a log file. Both were built for developing the package, and neither is needed to use it.","title":"Seeing what the package is doing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/functions/#Other-functions","page":"Other functions","text":"The remaining supported functions, as they are documented in the source. The functions meant for everyday use are on Main functions and types. A name that is not exported is written with the module name, as in ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options.\n\nprint_uncertainty_results and uq_reliability belong to the experimental uncertainty estimates. See Uncertainty.","title":"Other functions"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.create_ordered_ode_system-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"create_ordered_ode_system(name, states, parameters, equations, measured_quantities)\n\nCreate an OrderedODESystem with completed equations and ordered variables.\n\nArguments\n\nname: Name for the system\nstates: State variables\nparameters: System parameters\nequations: System equations\nmeasured_quantities: Equations for measured quantities\n\nReturns\n\nTuple of (OrderedODESystem, measured_quantities)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.create_ordered_ode_system"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options-Tuple{EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"merge_options(base::EstimationOptions; kwargs...) -> EstimationOptions\n\nCreate a new EstimationOptions struct by merging keyword arguments with an existing options struct. This is useful for temporarily overriding specific options.\n\nExamples\n\nbase_opts = EstimationOptions()\nnew_opts = ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options(base_opts; abstol = 1e-10, diagnostics = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.print_options-Tuple{IO, EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"print_options(io::IO, opts::EstimationOptions; compact=false)\n\nPrint every option and its value, grouped by topic. With compact = true, print only the options that differ from their defaults, and the defaults beside them; this is how EstimationOptions values show themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.print_options"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.print_uncertainty_results-Tuple{UncertaintyReport}","page":"Other functions","text":"print_uncertainty_results(uq::UncertaintyReport; io = stdout)\n\nPrint the standard errors of an UncertaintyReport, with its status and warnings. Experimental.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.print_uncertainty_results"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.uq_reliability-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Other functions","text":"uq_reliability(outcome) -> UQReliabilityAssessment\n\nThe separate checks on an uncertainty estimate: whether it was computed, whether its linear approximation passed the numerical checks, and how wide the intervals are. outcome is the third value of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem. Experimental. A single run cannot show whether the intervals cover the truth as often as they should, so that check always reads as not assessed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.uq_reliability"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.validate_options-Tuple{EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"validate_options(opts::EstimationOptions) -> Bool\n\nCheck the options. Each problem is logged: as an error when the options cannot work, as a warning when they work but are unwise.\n\nReturns\n\ntrue if the options can be used\nfalse if any of them cannot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.validate_options"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Contributing","page":"Contributing","text":"Bug reports and pull requests are welcome on GitHub.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Running-the-tests","page":"Contributing","text":"Add your checkout with Pkg.develop, then run these from Julia's default environment:\n\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl unit        # quick tests of the parts\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl             # everything\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl benchmark   # recovery from noisy data on full-size models\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl examples    # estimates the example models\n\nRun the full suite for any change that touches estimation. unit is for quick iteration and does not replace it.\n\nexamples estimates each model in ODEParameterEstimation.Examples with default options and checks the answer against test/example_expectations.jl. It takes about twenty minutes. A few models take many minutes each; those are in a group of their own, examples_slow, which estimates each model in a Julia process of its own. Both run weekly. Every push runs examples_ci, five models chosen in that file. A new example model needs an entry there.\n\nTo check that the package installs and passes with dependencies taken from the registry only, with none of your local development versions:\n\njulia --startup-file=no test/registered.jl","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Precompiling","page":"Contributing","text":"While the package is precompiled it estimates a small model, so that most of what estimate needs is compiled before anyone calls it. With that step, precompiling takes about six minutes every time src changes. Without it, it takes half a minute. To skip it while you work, put this in a LocalPreferences.toml beside the Project.toml of the environment you develop in:\n\n[ODEParameterEstimation]\nprecompile_workload = false\n\nJulia also reads that file from your default environment when another environment is active, so a setting left there reaches every environment that does not set its own.\n\nTake it out again before you time anything or run the tests for the last time. While it is there, they run on a package precompiled without that step.","title":"Precompiling"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Building-this-manual","page":"Contributing","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --startup-file=no --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe pages are in docs/src. Their examples run during the build, so a page that builds is a page whose code works. Open docs/build/index.html to read the result.","title":"Building this manual"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Writing-code","page":"Contributing","text":"Functions and variables are snake_case, types are PascalCase.\nTolerances are abstol and reltol. A function that takes the problem or the model takes it as its first argument.\nUse OrderedDict for parameters and states, so their order is fixed.\nGive each function a docstring with Arguments and Returns sections.\nA change a user can see needs its page in this manual and its docstring changed with it. ?EstimationOptions names every option, and a test checks that it does.","title":"Writing code"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Engineering-notes","page":"Contributing","text":"docs/internal has a few notes for people working on the package: how the polynomial systems are built and solved, why the precompile step and the column scaling work as they do, and where the code adapted from other packages came from.","title":"Engineering notes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#More-than-one-answer","page":"More than one answer","text":"For some models the data cannot single out one set of parameter values. That is a property of the model and of what was measured. It is not a failure of the fit, and more data or better data does not change it. There are two ways it happens, and the package reports both.","title":"More than one answer"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#Several-separate-answers","page":"More than one answer","text":"Two substances decay at their own rates, and only their total is measured:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y(t)\n@named decays = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x1, D(x2) ~ -b * x2], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(decays, [y ~ x1 + x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.5, b => 1.5, x1 => 2.0, x2 => 1.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 3.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n2-element Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}:\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 1.5, b = 0.5; x1(t) = 1, x2(t) = 2)\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 0.5, b = 1.5; x1(t) = 2, x2(t) = 1)\n\nThere are two results, and they are the same answer with the substances swapped:\n\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 1.5\n    b = 0.5\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 1\n    x2(t) = 2\n  Fit error: 2.77e-27\n\nresults[2]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.5\n    b = 1.5\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 2\n    x2(t) = 1\n  Fit error: 8.97e-27\n\nBoth reproduce the total exactly, and nothing in the data says which substance is which. If you know something more, for instance that the first one decays more slowly, that picks the answer. The package cannot know it, so it gives you every answer the data allow.","title":"Several separate answers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#Parameters-that-cannot-be-determined","page":"More than one answer","text":"In this model two rates only ever appear as a sum, and the third state is never measured:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b c d\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) x3(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named chain = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x1, D(x2) ~ (b + c) * x1, D(x3) ~ d * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(chain, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.1, b => 0.2, c => 0.3, d => 0.4, x1 => 2.0, x2 => 3.0, x3 => 4.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0])\n\nbest = estimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.1\n    c = 4  (not identifiable)\n    b = -3.5  (not identifiable)\n    d = 0.5  (not identifiable)\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 2\n    x2(t) = 3\n    x3(t) = 1.87578  (not identifiable)\n  Fit error: 1.01e-26\n\nThe data determine a, the two measured states, and the sum b + c:\n\nbest[b] + best[c]\n0.49999999999998934\n\nThey say nothing about how that sum splits into b and c, and nothing about d or x3. Those are marked not identifiable. To solve for everything else the package fixes each of them at some value, and that value is what is printed. It is arbitrary. Do not read anything into it.\n\nThe same information is available as a set:\n\nbest.all_unidentifiable\nSet{Symbolics.Num} with 4 elements:\n  x3(t)\n  d\n  c\n  b\n\nThere are two ways out. One is to measure more: with x3 measured as well, d can be found. The other is to say less: write the model with a single parameter in place of b + c, and it has one answer.\n\nStructuralIdentifiability.jl answers these questions for a model before any data exist. This package runs it for you at the start of every estimation.","title":"Parameters that cannot be determined"},{"category":"section","location":"#ODEParameterEstimation.jl","page":"Home","text":"ODEParameterEstimation.jl fits the parameters and initial conditions of an ODE model to time-series data. You give it the model and the measurements. It returns every parameter set that fits them, and tells you which parameters the data cannot determine.\n\nIt works by solving equations rather than by searching. Derivatives of the data are estimated, the model turns them into polynomial equations for the unknowns, and those equations are solved for all their solutions, which are then refined against the data. Because the equations must be polynomial, the model has to be built from polynomials and ratios of polynomials. That covers mass-action kinetics, population and epidemic models, compartment models and most linear systems.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = prey_counts, y2 = predator_counts))\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nGetting started runs this example and explains each line.","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Finding-your-way","page":"Home","text":"Tutorials each work through one situation: your own data, noisy data, models with more than one answer and uncertainty, which is experimental.\nGuides cover one topic each: which models work, the options worth knowing, how to read results, how the method works, and what to try when something goes wrong.\nReference documents the main functions and types.","title":"Finding your way"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"If you use this package in your work, please cite the papers it is based on:\n\nO. Bassik, A. Demin, A. Ovchinnikov. Practical algebraic parameter estimation for noisy data via Gaussian process regression. arXiv:2609.30451 (2026).\n\nO. Bassik, Y. Berman, S. Go, H. Hong, I. Ilmer, A. Ovchinnikov, C. Rackauckas, P. Soto, C. Yap. Robust parameter estimation for rational ordinary differential equations. Applied Mathematics and Computation 509 (2026). doi:10.1016/j.amc.2025.129638\n\nBibTeX entries are in CITATION.bib.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-first-call-is-slow","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"That is Julia compiling code for your model. It happens once for each model in a session, and the second call to estimate shows the real speed.","title":"The first call is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#It-is-still-running","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Pass progress = true to see which stage it is in. If it is working through the interpolators one after another, a shorter interpolators list or fewer shooting_points will cut the time. See Going faster. Large models can take a long time in any case. See Size.","title":"It is still running"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-estimates-are-off","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Simulate the model with the estimates and plot it against the data, as in Seeing the fit. Then:\n\nThe curve misses the data. With noisy data, more points help. See Noisy data. If the data are clean and the curve still misses, the model probably does not describe them.\nThe curve fits, but the values are not what you expected. The data may allow more than one answer. Look at the other results, and at whether any values are marked not identifiable. See More than one answer.","title":"The estimates are off"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#There-are-several-results,-or-values-marked-\"not-identifiable\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Both are the package telling you something about the model, and More than one answer explains what.","title":"There are several results, or values marked \"not identifiable\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#There-are-no-results","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"estimate returned an empty vector: none of the solutions of the equations held up when checked against the data. Check that the time points and values are what you meant to pass, and that the model can produce curves like them. Running with nooutput = false prints what each stage found.","title":"There are no results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#\"This-model-is-not-rational\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"The model applies a function such as sin or exp to a state or an unknown parameter. Which models work says what is allowed and how such a model can sometimes be rewritten.","title":"\"This model is not rational\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#\"This-model-needs-derivative-…-of-the-measured-data\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Too few quantities are measured for the number of unknowns. Measuring one more state usually brings the required derivatives down.","title":"\"This model needs derivative … of the measured data\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-results-change-from-run-to-run","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Several steps use random numbers. Pass a seed. See Repeatable results.","title":"The results change from run to run"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#Something-else","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Please open an issue with the model and, if you can share it, the data. A simulated data set that shows the same behavior is just as useful.","title":"Something else"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#Uncertainty","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"warning: Experimental\nUncertainty estimates are experimental. They have been tested on only a few models, and it is not yet known how well they hold up in general. If you try them, please open an issue and say how they did. Good or bad, that is useful to hear.\n\nWith noisy data an estimate is only good to within some margin. The package can estimate that margin, as a standard error for each parameter and initial condition.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.4, b => 0.8, x1 => 1.0, x2 => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem;\n    datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0], noise_level = 0.01, seed = 1)\n\nTwo options turn it on. compute_uncertainty = true asks for it. InterpolatorGPUncertainty is the curve fit that makes it possible: a Gaussian process that estimates the noise in the data along with the curve. The uncertainty comes back as the third value of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem:\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(interpolators = [InterpolatorGPUncertainty], compute_uncertainty = true, seed = 1)\nraw, analysis, uncertainty = analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, options)\n\nfor (name, value, standard_error) in zip(uncertainty.param_labels, uncertainty.estimate_values, uncertainty.param_std)\n    println(rpad(name, 4), round(value; sigdigits = 4), \" ± \", round(standard_error; sigdigits = 2))\nend\na   0.4007 ± 0.00049\nb   0.7976 ± 0.0019\nx1  1.001 ± 0.00083\nx2  0.5022 ± 0.0021\n\nThe data were simulated with a = 0.4, b = 0.8, x1 = 1 and x2 = 0.5, and each estimate is within a standard error or two of its true value.\n\nuncertainty.param_covariance is the full covariance matrix, and print_uncertainty_results(uncertainty) prints a longer report.","title":"Uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#What-the-numbers-mean","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"The Gaussian process learns how noisy the data are. That noise is then carried through the least-squares fit to the parameters, using a linear approximation around the estimate. The standard errors say how far the estimate would move if the same experiment were measured again with fresh noise.\n\nKeep three things in mind:\n\nThey belong to the first result. If the model has several answers, they say nothing about the others.\nThey rest on a linear approximation. With little noise it is good. With a lot of noise, or a model that reacts sharply to its parameters, treat them as a guide to the size of the error, not as exact confidence intervals.\nThey assume the model is right. If it does not describe the system, small standard errors do not make the estimates meaningful.","title":"What the numbers mean"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#When-it-cannot-be-computed","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"If the uncertainty cannot be worked out, the third value is an UncertaintyUnavailable in place of a report, and its message says why:\n\nif uncertainty isa UncertaintyReport\n    uncertainty.param_std\nelse\n    println(uncertainty.message)\nend\n\nA report also carries its own checks. uncertainty.status is :ok when they passed, and uncertainty.warnings lists the assumptions behind the numbers.","title":"When it cannot be computed"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Which-models-work","page":"Which models work","text":"","title":"Which models work"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#The-rule","page":"Which models work","text":"The right-hand sides of the model and the measured quantities have to be rational: built from the states, the parameters and numbers using +, -, *, / and whole-number powers.\n\nThat covers a lot of ground:\n\nmass-action chemistry, and anything else made of products of concentrations\npopulation and epidemic models such as Lotka–Volterra, SIR and SEIR\ncompartment models from pharmacokinetics\nsaturating terms such as V * x / (K + x), and Hill terms with whole-number exponents\nlinear systems\n\nParameters can go wherever states can: multiplied together, in denominators, and in the measured quantities.","title":"The rule"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Inputs-that-depend-on-time","page":"Which models work","text":"sin(c * t), cos(c * t) and exp(c * t) are accepted when c is a number. They depend on time alone, so they are known in advance:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x(t) y(t)\n@named forced = System([D(x) ~ -a * x + b * sin(0.5 * t)], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(forced, [y ~ x]; true_values = [a => 0.5, b => 2.0, x => 1.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 51, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0])\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.5\n    b = 2\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x(t) = 1\n  Fit error: 1.13e-25","title":"Inputs that depend on time"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#What-is-not-accepted","page":"Which models work","text":"Any other function of a state or of an unknown parameter: sin(θ), exp(-E / T), sqrt(h), log(x). The package refuses such a model before it does any work:\n\n@parameters g\n@variables θ(t) ω(t)\n@named pendulum = System([D(θ) ~ ω, D(ω) ~ -g * sin(θ)], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(pendulum, [y ~ θ]; data = (t = [0.0, 0.1, 0.2], y = [0.5, 0.49, 0.46]))\ntry\n    estimate(problem)\ncatch err\n    showerror(stdout, err)\nend\nThis model is not rational: it contains sin(θ(t)). A model must be built from polynomials and ratios of polynomials in its states and parameters. sin(c*t), cos(c*t) and exp(c*t) with a number c are accepted.\n\nThree more things are outside the rule: a frequency that is itself unknown, as in sin(w * t), a power with an unknown exponent, and time on its own, as in b * t.","title":"What is not accepted"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Rewriting-a-model-so-that-it-is-accepted","page":"Which models work","text":"Many models that break the rule can be rewritten to follow it by adding states. The idea is always the same: give the offending term a name, write down its derivative, and tell the package its values.\n\nA function of a measured state. In the pendulum, call sin(θ) and cos(θ) by the names s and c. Their derivatives are c * ω and -s * ω, which are polynomial. Since θ is measured, s and c are as good as measured too: their data are the sine and cosine of the angle data.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\n# Angles of a pendulum with g = 9.8, released from rest at 0.5 radians.\ntimes = collect(range(0, 2; length = 81))\nswing = solve(ODEProblem((u, p, t) -> [u[2], -9.8 * sin(u[1])], [0.5, 0.0], (0.0, 2.0)), Vern9(); saveat = times, abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)\nangles = swing[1, :]\n\n@variables s(t) c(t) ys(t) yc(t)\n@named pendulum = System([D(θ) ~ ω, D(ω) ~ -g * s, D(s) ~ c * ω, D(c) ~ -s * ω], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(pendulum, [y ~ θ, ys ~ s, yc ~ c];\n    data = (t = times, y = angles, ys = sin.(angles), yc = cos.(angles)))\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    g = 9.8\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    θ(t) = 0.5\n    ω(t) = 2.49024e-14\n    s(t) = 0.479426\n    c(t) = 0.877583\n  Fit error: 1.81e-25\n\nTime itself. Add a state τ with D(τ) ~ 1 and use it wherever the model has a bare t. Its data are the time points:\n\n@variables τ(t) yτ(t)\n@named ramp = System([D(x) ~ -a * x + b * τ, D(τ) ~ 1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(ramp, [y ~ x, yτ ~ τ];\n    data = (t = times, y = measurements, yτ = times))","title":"Rewriting a model so that it is accepted"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#What-has-to-be-measured","page":"Which models work","text":"Not every state needs to be measured. The states you do not measure are estimated along with the parameters, as initial conditions. What matters is that every unknown leaves a trace in what you measure. One that leaves none is reported as not identifiable. See More than one answer.\n\nThe fewer quantities you measure, the higher the derivatives of the data the method needs, and high derivatives are hard to get from noisy data. A model that needs more than the twentieth derivative is refused.","title":"What has to be measured"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Size","page":"Which models work","text":"The cost grows quickly with the number of unknowns, and it depends on the shape of the equations more than on the amount of data. Models with up to about a dozen parameters are the usual range. Larger ones can work, and take much longer. Example models lists some of each kind.","title":"Size"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/types/#Other-types","page":"Other types","text":"The option types and the types that come with a result, as they are documented in the source. The names meant for everyday use are on the previous page. A name that is not exported is written with the module name, as in ODEParameterEstimation.InterpolatorMethod.\n\nThe types to do with uncertainty, UncertaintyReport and those around it, belong to an experimental part of the package. See Uncertainty.","title":"Other types"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractUQOutcome","page":"Other types","text":"AbstractUQOutcome\n\nWhat analyze_parameter_estimation_problem returns as its third value when compute_uncertainty = true: an UncertaintyReport, or an UncertaintyUnavailable that says why there is none. Experimental.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractUQOutcome"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationFlow","page":"Other types","text":"EstimationFlow\n\nHow the estimate is made, chosen with the flow option.\n\nFlowStandard (default): the algebraic method.\nFlowDirectOpt: local optimization alone, from a random start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationFlow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.InterpolatorMethod","page":"Other types","text":"InterpolatorMethod\n\nThe curve fits that estimate the derivatives of the data, listed in the interpolators option.\n\nInterpolatorAAA: a rational approximation. Suits exact or nearly exact data.\nInterpolatorGP: a Gaussian process. Suits noisy data.\nInterpolatorGPUncertainty: a Gaussian process that also estimates the noise in the data. Needed for compute_uncertainty.\nInterpolatorCustom: a function of your own, from custom_interpolators.\n\nThe default list runs nine curve fits. The values not named here are internal and may change in any release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.InterpolatorMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.OrderedODESystem","page":"Other types","text":"OrderedODESystem\n\nA ModelingToolkit system together with its parameters and states in a fixed order. Results list their values in this order.\n\nThe keyword constructor of ParameterEstimationProblem builds one from a System, so you rarely need to.\n\nFields\n\nsystem: the ModelingToolkit system.\noriginal_parameters: the parameters, in order.\noriginal_states: the states, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.OrderedODESystem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.PolishMethod","page":"Other types","text":"PolishMethod\n\nThe optimizer that refines each solution against the data, chosen with the polish_method option.\n\nPolishLeastSquares (default): Levenberg–Marquardt on the residuals.\nPolishBFGS, PolishLBFGS: minimize the sum of squares directly, with the Optim.jl method of that name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.PolishMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.ResultProvenance","page":"Other types","text":"ResultProvenance\n\nHow a ParameterEstimationResult was produced, in its provenance field.\n\nFields\n\nprimary_method: :algebraic when the result came from solving the polynomial equations, :direct_opt when it came from fitting the model directly.\ninterpolator_source: the curve fit whose derivative estimates led to it, as a short label, or nothing.\nrescue_path: :none, or the recovery that produced it: :algebraic_resolve_t0, :algebraic_resolve_shoot or :direct_opt_fallback.\nsource_shooting_index: which of the time points chosen by shooting_points the equations were solved at.\nsource_candidate_index: its position among the candidates of that stage.\npre_polish_error, post_polish_error: the fit error before and after refinement, when it was refined.\npolish_applied: whether it was refined.\nrepresentative_assignments: values given to quantities only because the data cannot determine them.\nstructural_fix_set: the values the undeterminable quantities were fixed at to make the equations solvable.\ntemplate_status: whether, after that, the equations had as many unknowns as equations.\nequations_dropped_by_rank_trimming: equations left out because they were dependent on the others.\npractical_identifiability_status: a numerical check of how well the data determine the values.\nnumerical_advisory: further numerical diagnostics, or nothing. Its type is internal.\nnotes: short labels for anything else that happened along the way.\n\nThe remaining fields are internal bookkeeping and may change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ResultProvenance"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.SystemSolverMethod","page":"Other types","text":"SystemSolverMethod\n\nHow the polynomial systems are solved, chosen with the system_solver option.\n\nSolverHC (default): homotopy continuation, which finds every solution.\nSolverTrustRegion: a trust-region method started from several points.\nSolverLevenbergMarquardt: Levenberg–Marquardt from one point.\n\nThe last two are local solvers and can miss solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.SystemSolverMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQReliabilityAssessment","page":"Other types","text":"UQReliabilityAssessment\n\nThe separate checks on an uncertainty estimate, as returned by uq_reliability. Experimental. Each field is a symbol:\n\navailability: :available, :unavailable, or :not_requested.\nnumerical_linearization: :accepted when the linear approximation passed its numerical checks, :degraded when it did not.\ninterval_width: :narrow when every standard error is under half its estimate, :wide when under twice, :extreme beyond that; :invalid or :undefined_scale when they cannot be compared.\nselection_scope: which estimate the standard errors belong to.\nempirical_calibration: always :not_assessed_by_single_run for a report. Whether the intervals cover the truth as often as they should can only be judged over many runs.\n\nUncertaintyReport's status sums these up in one value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQReliabilityAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyError","page":"Other types","text":"UncertaintyError\n\nThrown instead of returning an UncertaintyUnavailable when the uncertainty cannot be worked out and the options say uq_failure_policy = :throw. Its outcome field is that UncertaintyUnavailable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyReport","page":"Other types","text":"UncertaintyReport\n\nStandard errors for the best result, the third value of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem when compute_uncertainty = true. Experimental; see Uncertainty. The noise that the Gaussian-process curve fit finds in the data is carried through to the estimates by a linear approximation around them.\n\nFields\n\nparam_labels: the names of the parameters and initial conditions, in order.\nestimate_values: their estimated values.\nparam_std: their standard errors.\nparam_covariance: the covariance matrix, in the same order.\ncorrelation_matrix: the same, as correlations.\nstatus: :ok when every standard error is under half its estimate, :wide_ci when under twice, :degenerate beyond that or when the numerical checks failed. uq_reliability gives the checks separately.\nwarnings: the assumptions behind the numbers, and anything that went wrong.\n\nThe other fields record how the numbers were computed and may change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyUnavailable","page":"Other types","text":"UncertaintyUnavailable\n\nReturned in place of an UncertaintyReport when the uncertainty was asked for but cannot be worked out. Experimental. message says why, reason says it as a symbol, and warnings lists anything else that went wrong. The other fields are internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyUnavailable"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Your-own-data","page":"Your own data","text":"To estimate from measurements you already have, hand them to the problem as data. Nothing else changes.","title":"Your own data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Measurements-in-vectors","page":"Your own data","text":"Take a model with two states, both measured, and two unknown rates:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\nmeasured = [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2]\n\nMeasurements are a vector of times and one vector of values for each measured quantity. These three stand in for yours:\n\ntimes = collect(range(0, 5; length = 41))\nseries1 = cos.(0.6 .* times .+ 0.3)\nseries2 = 1.5 .* sin.(0.6 .* times .+ 0.3)\n\nGive them under the names on the left-hand sides of the measured quantities, with the times under t:\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured;\n    data = (t = times, y1 = series1, y2 = series2))\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 0.955336\n    x2(t) = 0.44328\n  Fit error: 7.34e-26\n\nThose two curves solve the model for a = 0.4 and b = 0.9, which is what came back, along with their values at the first time point.","title":"Measurements in vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Measurements-in-a-table","page":"Your own data","text":"data can be anything with named columns, such as a DataFrame read from a file:\n\nusing CSV, DataFrames\n\ntable = CSV.read(\"measurements.csv\", DataFrame)   # columns t, y1 and y2\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured; data = table)\n\nOther columns are ignored, and the rows do not have to be in time order. A dictionary works too: Dict(\"t\" => times, \"y1\" => series1, \"y2\" => series2).\n\nEvery series needs a value at every time. If the table has gaps, either drop those rows (dropmissing(table)) or describe each series separately, as below.","title":"Measurements in a table"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Series-measured-at-different-times","page":"Your own data","text":"When the quantities were not measured at the same times, describe each series on its own with an ObservationSeries and collect them in an ObservationData:\n\ntimes1 = collect(range(0, 5; length = 41))\ntimes2 = collect(range(0.1, 4.9; length = 33))\n\ndata = ObservationData([\n    ObservationSeries(\"y1\", \"run 1\", x1, times1, cos.(0.6 .* times1 .+ 0.3)),\n    ObservationSeries(\"y2\", \"run 1\", x2, times2, 1.5 .* sin.(0.6 .* times2 .+ 0.3)),\n])\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured; data)\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 0.955336\n    x2(t) = 0.44328\n  Fit error: 2.29e-26\n\nEach series takes a name, a name for the experiment it belongs to, the quantity that was measured, its times and its values. The quantity is the right-hand side of the measured quantity, x1 and not y1. Initial conditions are reported at time zero unless you pass initial_time to ObservationData.","title":"Series measured at different times"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#How-much-data-is-enough","page":"Your own data","text":"The method works from the shape of each curve, so it needs enough points to see that shape: a few dozen per series, spread over a stretch of time in which the system is doing something. A long tail where everything has settled adds little.","title":"How much data is enough"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#If-the-measurements-are-noisy","page":"Your own data","text":"The example above used exact numbers. Real measurements are not exact, and estimate allows for that: it fits its answers to the data before returning them. Noisy data shows how much that matters, and what else helps.","title":"If the measurements are noisy"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Checking-the-result","page":"Your own data","text":"A result holds numbers, not a judgement of whether the model is right. Simulate the model with the estimates and look at it against the measurements, as in Seeing the fit. If the curve misses the data, the model does not describe them, whatever the parameters.","title":"Checking the result"}]
}
